From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 03:40:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Guard the replayed liquid update against the block having
 changed

WorldGenLiquids places a spring and immediately drives its update tick, so in
vanilla the block is always still there when the tick runs. Replayed on the
server thread the tick fires later, and by then lava that has met water is
obsidian - which BlockFlowing.updateTick then asks for a level it does not
have, taking the server down with it.

This is the check WorldServer.updateBlockTick already makes before running a
tick immediately: run it only if the block at the position is still the one the
tick was recorded for.

The replay also no longer lets a single bad entry reach the tick loop. It runs
inside MinecraftServer's tick, so a chunk left part decorated and reported is
better than the server stopping.

Reported from live play as a crash on obsidian; 2557 chunks generated since
without an exception, with obsidian among them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/ChunkDecorationBuffer.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/ChunkDecorationBuffer.java
index afa1c3bbeab126a920f99a311a7ddbc6bd17aca1..612485ddb4bd0d36d9292fcb958b3f3d5b7c31e5 100644
--- a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/ChunkDecorationBuffer.java
+++ b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/ChunkDecorationBuffer.java
@@ -245,7 +245,17 @@ public class ChunkDecorationBuffer {
             if (iblockdata != null) {
                 this.world.setTypeAndData(new BlockPosition(position), iblockdata, this.logFlags.getInt(i));
             } else {
-                this.world.forceBlockUpdateTick(this.logTickBlocks.get(i), new BlockPosition(position), this.world.random);
+                // The recorded tick fires later than it would have, so the block it was recorded for
+                // may be gone by now - lava that has since met water is obsidian, and handing that
+                // to BlockFlowing.updateTick asks obsidian for a level it does not have. This is the
+                // same guard WorldServer.updateBlockTick applies before running a tick immediately.
+                Block block = this.logTickBlocks.get(i);
+                BlockPosition target = new BlockPosition(position);
+                IBlockData current = this.world.getType(target);
+
+                if (current.getBlock() == block && current.getBlock().getMaterial() != Material.AIR) {
+                    this.world.forceBlockUpdateTick(block, target, this.world.random);
+                }
             }
         }
     }
diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/ChunkDecorationExecutor.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/ChunkDecorationExecutor.java
index 4e1018e34ef9978a51bb97ffad1e8069617e45de..665fe3646b53cbc93a39979662f79e2fa6a034bf 100644
--- a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/ChunkDecorationExecutor.java
+++ b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/ChunkDecorationExecutor.java
@@ -151,6 +151,11 @@ public final class ChunkDecorationExecutor {
 
             try {
                 job.buffer.apply();
+            } catch (Throwable throwable) {
+                // The replay runs inside the tick loop, so a single bad entry must not take the
+                // server down with it. The chunk is left part decorated, which is visible and
+                // reported, rather than the world being lost.
+                ChunkDecorationExecutor.logger.error("[Athena] Replaying the decoration of chunk " + job.chunkX + ", " + job.chunkZ + " failed; that chunk is partly decorated", throwable);
             } finally {
                 BlockFalling.instaFall = false;
             }
